## Commands

| Task          | Command          |
| ------------- | ---------------- |
| Install       | `pnpm install`   |
| Run (dev)     | `pnpm dev`       |
| Lint          | `pnpm lint`      |
| Type check    | `pnpm typecheck` |
| Build         | `pnpm build`     |
| Run the build | `pnpm start`     |
| Write the migrations | `pnpm db:generate` |
| Apply the migrations | `pnpm db:migrate`  |
| Add the seed records | `pnpm db:seed`     |

## Database

The app uses Postgres with Drizzle ORM. Copy `.env.example` to
`.env.local`, and set `DATABASE_URL`. The schema is in
`src/shared/db/schema/`. `pnpm db:generate` writes the SQL migrations
into `drizzle/`: keep them in version control. With
`DATABASE_MIGRATE=true`, the server applies them when it starts.

### Seed data

`pnpm db:seed` fills the tables of `DATABASE_URL` with seed records,
for example `DATABASE_URL=pglite:./.pglite pnpm db:seed`. The records
come from the app definition, and are the same on each run. Apply the
migrations first. The seed adds each record of the `seed` section of
the definition that the database does not have (by ID), and does not
change a record that it has. It adds the generated mock records only
if all tables are empty. With `DATABASE_SEED=true`, the server
runs the seed when it starts, after the migrations.
`src/shared/db/seed-data.ts` holds the records.

### Local database

To run the app without a Postgres server, use PGlite, an embedded
Postgres that runs in the Node.js process. Set these values in
`.env.local`, then run `pnpm db:generate` and `pnpm dev`:

```sh
DATABASE_URL=pglite:./.pglite
DATABASE_MIGRATE=true
```

PGlite keeps the data in the folder `.pglite/`. It uses the same schema
and the same migrations as Postgres. Only one process can open the
folder: stop the server before you run `pnpm db:migrate`. Use Postgres
in production.

## Docker

`docker compose up --build` starts Postgres and the app on
<http://localhost:3000>. The app applies the migrations and adds the
seed records when it starts.
The `Dockerfile` builds a standalone Next.js server that runs as a
non-root user. If `drizzle/` does not exist, the build writes the first
migration from the schema. `node migrate.mjs` in the image applies the
migrations and stops.

{% if nextjs.jobs %}
## Jobs

A scheduler calls the route `POST /api/jobs/<job>` of each job, with
the header `Authorization: Bearer <JOBS_SECRET>`. A call runs the runs
of the job that are due in the last `JOBS_WINDOW_MINUTES` (60), in
UTC. The deployment files have a CronJob for each job with a schedule,
and one each minute for the jobs at the time of a date field. Compose
starts the service `jobs`, which calls each route each minute.

| Job | When | What |
| --- | ---- | ---- |
{% for job in nextjs.jobs.values() %}
| `{{ job.name }}` | {{ job.when }} | {% if job.entity %}The operation `{{ job.operation }}` on each {{ job.entity }} record of the filter{% elif job.builtin %}The daily values of the stat widgets with `snapshots: daily` (built in){% else %}`src/app/jobs/{{ job.module }}.ext.ts`{% endif %} |
{% endfor %}

- A run is for one record (or for none) and one time. The table
  `job_runs` keeps each run with its state, and its key locks the run:
  each run runs one time, also with two calls at the same time or more
  than one replica.
- A call runs only the times of the window. A late call runs the same
  runs, and a second call runs none again. After a stop of the
  scheduler, the times before the window do not run.
- A failed run rolls back its changes, goes into the log (a JSON line
  with the job and the record), and runs again at the next call while
  its time is in the window. The route then gives 500, with the counts
  of the runs.
- Without the secret, the route gives 401. Without `JOBS_SECRET`, each
  call gives 401.
- `JOBS_FIXED_CLOCK=true` lets the parameter `now` set the time of a
  call, for the tests:
  `curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $JOBS_SECRET" "http://localhost:3000/api/jobs/<job>?now=2026-06-15T12:00:00Z"`.
  Do not set it in production.

