## Production

{% set name = app.name ~ "-web" %}
- Health: `/health` gives `{"status":"ok"}` while the database answers,
  else 503. The probes of the deployment files use it.
- Rate limits: `RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE` sets the number of requests that
  each client address can send in each minute (0, the default, sets no
  limit). `proxy.ts` counts the pages, the server actions, and the route
  handlers, but not the static files and `/health`. Over the limit, the
  app gives 429 with `Retry-After`. A page view makes 10 to 25 requests
  (the page, the prefetches of its links, and the data of its views), so
  the deployment files set 1200, and Compose sets no limit.
{% if nextjs.redis %}
  The counts are in the memory of each process, or in Redis with
  `RATE_LIMIT_STORE_URL` (for example `redis://localhost:6379/0`): then
  all replicas share one limit. When Redis is not available, the app
  logs a warning and lets the requests through, or gives 503 with
  `RATE_LIMIT_FAIL_CLOSED=true`. Compose starts Redis with
  `--profile redis`.
{% else %}
  The counts are in the memory of each process, so each replica has its
  own limit.
{% endif %}
- The client address: the app counts by the address that the proxy in
  front of the app (an ingress, a load balancer, or Cloud Run) adds to
  the end of `X-Forwarded-For`. `RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_HOPS` (default 1)
  is the number of addresses that your proxies add: set 2 behind a load
  balancer of Google Cloud, which adds the address of the client and its
  own address. Clients must reach the app only through the proxy, else
  a client can send any `X-Forwarded-For`.
