  kubectl apply -f deploy/knative/service.yaml
{% if nextjs.jobs %}
  kubectl apply -f deploy/knative/jobs.yaml
{% endif %}
  ```

  On Cloud Run, a Cloud Run job applies the migrations. The database
  must accept connections from Cloud Run, and the service account of the
  service and of the job must have access to the secrets:

  ```sh
{% for env in secrets %}
  printf %s '<{{ env }}>' | gcloud secrets create {{ name }}-{{ env | lower | replace("_", "-") }} --data-file=-
{% endfor %}
  gcloud run jobs deploy {{ name }}-migrate --image {{ image }} \
    --command node --args migrate.mjs \
    --set-secrets DATABASE_URL={{ name }}-database-url:latest --execute-now --wait
  gcloud run services replace deploy/knative/service.yaml
  ```

  A new Cloud Run service accepts only requests with a Google sign-in.
  For a public app, run `gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding
  {{ name }} --member=allUsers --role=roles/run.invoker`.
{% if nextjs.jobs %}

  Cloud Run has no CronJobs: Cloud Scheduler calls the route of each
  job. The route reads the secret from the header `Authorization`, so
  the service must accept requests without a Google sign-in (see
  above). A request of Cloud Run can take at most `timeoutSeconds` (60
  in `service.yaml`): the runs that do not start in that time run at the
  next call.

  ```sh
  url=$(gcloud run services describe {{ name }} --format 'value(status.url)')
{% for job in nextjs.jobs.values() %}
  gcloud scheduler jobs create http {{ name }}-{{ job.module }} --schedule "{{ job.schedule.expression if job.schedule else "* * * * *" }}" \
    --time-zone Etc/UTC --http-method POST --uri "$url/api/jobs/{{ job.name }}" \
    --headers "Authorization=Bearer <JOBS_SECRET>"
{% endfor %}
  ```
