const client = {{ (ts_apis.values() | first).factory }}();
```

{% if ts_connections %}
### Connections of the users

The clients of {% for api in ts_connections %}`{{ api.name }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} send the access token of a user: the
authorization code flow of OAuth 2.0. A signed-in user connects an account of the service on the page
`/connections` (`src/shared/auth/connections.ts`):

1. `/connections/<api>/start` sends the browser to the authorization
   URL of the service, with a signed cookie for the flow.
2. The user allows the access, and the service sends the browser back
   to `/connections/<api>/callback`. The app changes the code for the
   tokens, and sends the browser to `/connections`.

Register `<PUBLIC_URL>/connections/<api>/callback` as the redirect URI
of the app at the service, and set `PUBLIC_URL` to the URL of the app
for the browsers. The table `api_connections` keeps the tokens of each
user, encrypted with a key from `AUTH_SECRET`: a new `AUTH_SECRET` ends
the connections. The client gets a new access token with the refresh
token when the token ends. `{{ (ts_connections | first).factory }}()` uses the signed-in
user, and a server action gives an error result if the user has no
connection. Other code gets a client with
`{{ (ts_connections | first).client_for }}(userId)`.

{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if ts_webhooks %}
## Webhooks

Each webhook of the definition has a route handler `app/<path>/route.ts`
and a feature slice in `src/features/<webhook>-webhook/`: the function
that receives the request (`api/<webhook>-webhook.gen.ts`), the logic
(`api/<webhook>-webhook.ext.ts`), and the types and zod schemas of the
schema of the body (`model/event.ts`). The endpoint checks the
signature of the raw body with the secret, and gives 401 for a wrong
signature. The table `webhook_events` keeps the ID of each event, so a
repeated delivery gives `"repeated": true` and does not run the logic
again. The app adds the row before the logic runs: when the logic
throws, the app removes the row, and the sender can deliver the event
again. The changes that the logic made before the error stay.

| Webhook | Endpoint | Signature | Secret |
| ------- | -------- | --------- | ------ |
{% for w in ts_webhooks.values() %}
| `{{ w.name }}` | `POST {{ w.path }}` | {{ w.signature_text }} | `{{ w.secret_env }}` |
{% endfor %}
{% if ts_webhooks.values() | selectattr("email") | list %}

A webhook with the format `email` receives the inbound emails that a
mail service forwards (Mailgun, Postmark, or SendGrid). The logic gets
the email as `InboundEmail` of `@/shared/webhooks`: the sender, the receivers, the subject, the
text, the `In-Reply-To` header, and the names and sizes of the
attachments. The event ID is the `Message-ID` header. With HTTP Basic,
the secret is `<user name>:<password>`, and the URL of the webhook at
the mail service has them, for example
`https://<user name>:<password>@example.com/webhooks/support`.
{% endif %}

Without its secret, an endpoint gives 500. A `WebhookError` of
`@/shared/webhooks` in the logic gives its status to the sender.

{% endif %}
