{% else %}
The app keeps the files of the file fields in the folder of
`{{ st.folder_env }}` (default `uploads`). The Docker image has the folder
`/app/uploads`, and `compose.yaml` keeps it in a volume.
{% endif %}

{% for entity_name, entity in definition.entities.items() %}
{% for field_name, field in entity.file_fields.items() %}
- `{{ entity_name }}.{{ field_name }}`: at most {{ size_text(field.size_limit) }}{% if field.media_types %}, the types {% for t in field.media_types %}`{{ t }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}{% endif %}.
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

The data access `<entity>Files` of each entity with file fields uploads
a file (`upload(id, field, file)`) and deletes it (`remove(id, field)`)
with the server actions of the entity. They need the permission of the
update operation of the entity, else of its create operation. A new file
replaces the old one. A file that is too large, or a type that the field
does not allow, gives a `ValidationError`. The app takes the type from
the first bytes of a PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, or PDF file, else from the
browser. `next.config.ts` allows server action bodies of the largest file
and 1 MB more.

A record gives each file as `name`, `size`, `type`, and `url`. The URL
is the route `/files/<entity>/<id>/<field>`, which checks the session
and the access rules of the read of the record, as a read does. The
delete of a record deletes its files. The files of the records that the
database deletes with `on_delete: cascade` stay in the storage.

